PlanRepository (tests/domain/plan/plan.ts) now exposes its DDD Repository tactic as collection-typed accessors — plans(): Promise<Collection<Plan>> and availablePlans(): Promise<Collection<Plan>> (keyed by plan name, via a shared MapCollection<T> in tests/domain/shared/collection.ts) — alongside the finder methods findAvailable(filter) and findById(id), which stay as direct methods since finders/queries are not themselves the collection. All methods are async (return Promise<T>) because the e2e tier (plan.e2e.ts) reads live Playwright/DOM state on every call rather than a pre-fetched snapshot — Playwright's page.evaluate() is inherently I/O-bound, so a domain repository interface backed by it cannot be truly synchronous; Promise<T> plus await at call sites is the correct, unavoidable shape (confirmed end-to-end against the live sign-up sandbox, tests passing). General guidance for future DDD tactics: encode the tactic's proper shape explicitly per pattern — Repository as a collection interface (get/has/keys/values + finder methods), Aggregate Root as the sole mutation entry point, Value Object as immutable — rather than letting each be modeled as ad hoc methods.